  abstract = {We give a two-dimensional (2-D) velocity-stress finite-difference\textbackslash n(vs-fd) formulation for the multipole borehole acoustic logging problem.\textbackslash nIrregular boreholes, horizontal bedding, and axially varying alternation\textbackslash nare encompassed by the model. Excellent agreement is obtained with\textbackslash ntransform techniques for constant radius boreholes and homogeneous\textbackslash nformations. The method is used to generate waveforms for many positions\textbackslash nof transmitter and receiver array relative to beds and washouts as in\textbackslash nlogging. First motion and semblance techniques are applied to the\textbackslash nwave-forms to extract formation slownesses, resulting in synthetic\textbackslash nmultiple logs which demonstrate the response of ideal multipole logging\textbackslash ntools and signal processing to several environmental effects.\textbackslash nFor monopole, compressional, first-motion logs, residual slowness errors\textbackslash nremain at borehole washouts after borehole compensation. These errors\textbackslash nincrease as the effective measure point in the waveforms is moved back\textbackslash nin time from true first motion, admitting greater interference from\textbackslash nreflected and mode-converted waves. Errors at bed boundaries are\textbackslash ntypically smaller than those at washouts. Stoneley slowness logs are\textbackslash nobtained by narrow-band filtering of low frequency monopole waveforms\textbackslash nand application of the semblance slowness extraction algorithm STC. \textbackslash nSimilar processing applied to low frequency dipole waveforms yields\textbackslash nflexural mode slowness logs. A small correction satisfactorily accounts\textbackslash nfor flexural mode dispersion properties, yielding formation shear\textbackslash nslowness. Slowness errors for both Stoneley and dipole shear logs at\textbackslash nwashouts and bed boundaries are quite small, typically on the order of a\textbackslash nfew percent. Borehole compensation is of marginal benefit for both of\textbackslash nthese logs since they are based on modes of the borehole rather than\textbackslash nheadwaves.},
