  abstract = {The popularity of electronic sports (esports) has been rapidly increasing over the recent years. Yet recent discussions have regarded esports as a threat to psychological and physical health and cognitive abilities of esports players. The present study was conducted to investigate the effects of esports training on cognitive abilities in esports players’ planning and decision-making. Physical education undergraduate students (40 participants, mean age = 20.20 ± 0.75 years, all male) who had not previously played the video game FIFA were selected and randomly assigned to two groups, namely the game group and the non-game group. Planning ability was measured using Tower of London (TOL) task and decision-making skill was assessed using the Iowa Gambling Task (IGT). The results indicated that the participants in the game group outperformed the participants in the non-game group in terms of both planning and decision-making skills. Based on the results of this study, esports training can improve the cognitive skills of decision-making and planning. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)},
